+## Design Notes
+
+- **Why windowed (and why NOT the 3.4 latches):** the 3.4 cumulative loss latch is
+  MONOTONE by contract ("a breached loss class stays breached until a windowed
+  measurement replaces this one — slice-4 health, E30" — pinned in sla_evaluate's own
+  comment). Feeding the meter from it would drain forever after the first breach: every
+  run would lose. The health layer's rolling window (the prototype's proven
+  `rolling_window_ticks`, 10 s at 20 Hz) is the promised replacement seam — it forgives
+  the build phase and makes recovery real.
+- **Why per-class (v2 arch letter) over the prototype's overall meter:** the v2 arch §6.5
+  is the standing bar — "drains while ANY ACTIVE CLASS's SLA is breached",
+  "enter/exit thresholds PER CLASS (catalog values)", E30 "one countdown per episode".
+  The prototype's single global meter (overall uptime < 90) is the earlier design; the
+  per-class model carries the same numbers + mechanics (the prototype's per-class
+  rolling windows already existed) and satisfies the arch. The WIN stays overall
+  (E6.1: "per-class AND OVERALL, sustained over the surge window" — the win row is the
+  overall one).
+- **Why per-class catalog severity (not the prototype's magnitude formula):** the
+  prototype computed `drain = clamp((90 − uptime)/10, 1, 2)`. The v2 arch pins only
+  "max(severity) + per-tick cap"; per-class catalog values (ODN-5: numbers single-
+  source) are the tunable, testable form — email 1, streaming 2 reproduce the
+  prototype's max drain 2/tick exactly. "Rate ∝ severity" holds: the harder class
+  drains faster.
+- **The never-lose exploit + the gradual refill:** without refill, a flapping line
+  (breach → recover → breach) gets a fresh FULL grace every episode and the meter never
+  drains — the Godot-prototype exploit the Odin prototype's review W1 killed. The
+  per-class `grace_left` persists across episodes and refills only +5/tick after a
+  50-tick healthy streak — consecutive episodes burn the shared countdown. Carried
+  verbatim in spirit (per-class).
+- **The win demo fixture is sized by MEASUREMENT, not guesswork:** the real surge is
+  20 streaming/tick × 60 units = 1200 u/tick; wide = 40 u/tick; the 4-port router cap
+  bounds bundles to ≤ 4 pipes/router. The candidate fixture: 15 routers × (2-wide
+  host→rt + 2-wide rt→res) = 60 wide pipes ≈ 1200 u/tick per leg — measured against the
+  actual sim and adjusted until the windowed uptime clears 90% with margin (the 
+  field-note: reported success lies — read the sim's own numbers).
+- **FLAGGED tuning risk (recorded, not re-tuned):** the era-3 session demand scale
+  (120 u/tick base, 1200 u/tick surge) vs the 20-Hz pipe capacities (wide 40 u/tick)
+  makes the app session a hard build-race: ~3 wides pre-surge + ~30 wides during the
+  surge (the surge window + grace/drain ≈ 100 s of reaction). The win IS achievable
